# applications/knowledge/platform/env/knowledge-api/base.env
#
# The Process scope: what only this Process receives
# (docs/adr/model/0011-configuration-env-files-per-process.md). What both
# Processes receive is one directory up, in env/_applications/knowledge/base.env.
# Literals are literal. Derived values are named placeholders. A literal for a
# derived key is a build error, and so is any OTEL_* or PYROSCOPE_* key --
# those come from runtime: jvm.

# --- app knobs
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod
KB_RECALL_DEFAULT_MODE=hybrid
KNOWLEDGE_MODE=lite

# --- this Process's own secret: the API serves /mcp and the worker does not, so
#     the grant is on the Process and so is the reference that binds it.
#     The string between ${secret: and # is the same string the grant writes as
#     `path:` -- mount and KV `data/` segment included -- so
#     E_UNAUTHORISED_SECRET_REFERENCE and the dead-grant check are string
#     comparisons over the composed union
#     (docs/adr/model/0027-secret-reference-join-key.md). The `#<key>` half
#     selects which value fills the variable and confers nothing: the grant
#     unit is the path.
KNOWLEDGE_MCP_TOKENS_WORKSTATION=${secret:secret/data/knowledge-system/mcp-bearer#workstation}

# The placeholder names the source; the key names the variable. That is what
# lets knowledge write DB_USER where another consumer of the same Postgres
# writes DB_POSTGRESDB_USER. Renaming a path edits the grant and every
# placeholder naming it in lockstep -- which is also what made the
# platform/postgres split a mechanical change: one grep finds every reader.

# NOT here, and a build error if they were:
#   SERVER_PORT             -> from `provides` on this Process
#   DEPLOYMENT_ENVIRONMENT  -> from the Cluster Target
#   OTEL_* (10 keys)        -> from runtime: jvm
#   PYROSCOPE_* (6 keys)    -> from runtime: jvm
#
# production.env sits alongside this file and is currently empty: nothing
# differs for the production cluster.
